    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "gnats? lol okay, i guess that's one way to put it. but gnats are just annoying, they don't really \"attack\" your circuit logic. \n\nwhat i mean is, it feels less like random noise and more like there's a pattern to the chaos. like, why does the interference always seem to happen at the most critical part of the signal? it's like these things know exactly where the vulnerabilities are. is there any concept in engineering for this kind of \"intelligent\" or targeted interference, or am i just losing my mind from the stress?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are definitely not losing your mind, though the stress of debugging these subtle failures is enough to make anyone feel like they're being targeted by a rogue intelligence. The feeling that the interference is \"intelligent\" is a common psychological response when dealing with highly non-linear and intermittent failures. In engineering, we don't typically call it \"intelligent interference,\" but rather we look at phenomena like resonance, crosstalk, or timing margins collapsing under specific load conditions.\n\nWhen interference consistently hits the most critical part of the signal, it suggests that your design is operating too close to a threshold—a point where a small, seemingly insignificant perturbation causes a massive, disproportionate failure. This is where the system's inherent vulnerabilities are exposed. It's not a conscious attack, but rather the system's sensitivity being pushed past its operational limits by the slightest environmental nudge.\n\nTo get a handle on this, you need to move beyond just looking at the signal amplitude and start looking at the *timing* and *coupling* between components. Could you be experiencing a specific mode of crosstalk where the noise couples only when a specific high-frequency transition occurs on an adjacent line? That's the engineering equivalent of a highly organized, yet entirely unintentional,",
      "source": "target",
